# weak_cycle_break.tkr — the COL-F0d `weak_get` fixture (crumb 0024). `main.tks` models a
# two-object cycle (`a` strongly holds `b`; `b`'s back-edge to `a` is realized as a `weak_get`
# read, never a `retain`). Exits 0 only if: `weak_get` on `a` is live while its count is still > 0;
# releasing the external hold on `a` and the strong `a`→`b` edge drives BOTH counts to zero
# (`weak_get` on both then returns `null`); and both region headers are actually recycled by a
# fresh `region_new` (the LIFO free-list only returns an address once it has been freed) — proving
# the weak edge let the whole pair be collected instead of leaking a strong cycle. The compiler
# holds no `weak_get` callers, so this path is not fixpoint-exercised. Pinned to the C backend:
# `weak_get` emits on the C route only.

Feature: weak_cycle_break — a weak back-edge lets a two-object cycle fully free

  Scenario: releasing_the_strong_chain_frees_both_objects
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
